  "beats": [
    {
      "beat_id": "B00",
      "narration_text": "Hi, my name is Ushasvi Rachel, and this video is about model calibration and whether we can trust a machine-learning model’s confidence scores.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "drawon",
        "visual_intent": "Presenter introduction and confidence framing.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "intro"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B00.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B00.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.0,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B00.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "narration_text": "If a model says it is 90 percent confident, should it actually be correct about 90 percent of the time? That question is about calibration—not just whether one prediction happens to be right.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "kinetic",
        "visual_intent": "Large 90 percent confidence meter and conceptual question.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "question"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.39,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "narration_text": "A well-calibrated classifier produces probabilities that correspond reasonably well to observed frequencies. Across many predictions near 80 percent, roughly 80 percent of those outcomes should occur. That is a group-level interpretation, not a guarantee for an individual case or tiny sample.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "drawon",
        "visual_intent": "Predicted 80 percent compared with observed frequency.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "definition"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.29,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "narration_text": "Prediction and probability are different. The prediction is the class or outcome the model chooses. Confidence is how strongly the model estimates that outcome. We can evaluate the chosen class and the attached probability separately.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 19,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "branch",
        "visual_intent": "Prediction versus probability comparison.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "prediction"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.53,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "narration_text": "Accuracy asks how often class predictions are correct. Calibration asks whether probability values match observed frequencies. A model may classify many examples correctly while assigning probabilities that are systematically too high or too low.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "divergence",
        "visual_intent": "Large Accuracy not equal Calibration comparison.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "different"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B04.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.89,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "narration_text": "An overconfident model might repeatedly report probabilities near 95 percent while those predictions are correct much less frequently. The problem is not only whether each class prediction was right or wrong. The confidence estimate itself is unreliable.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 19,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "scale",
        "visual_intent": "Illustrative 95 percent confidence versus lower observed correctness.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "overconfident"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B05.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.81,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "narration_text": "Underconfidence goes the other direction. A model may be correct very frequently while assigning probabilities that are consistently more cautious than the observed outcomes justify. Its probabilities understate how often comparable predictions succeed.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "scale",
        "visual_intent": "Underconfidence confidence meter and observed-frequency contrast.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "underconfident"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B06.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.77,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "narration_text": "Calibration matters because confidence can determine automatic acceptance, human review, risk thresholds, ranking, prioritization, and downstream actions. A poorly calibrated score can send the same prediction into the wrong workflow even when the predicted class is unchanged.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 20,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "threshold",
        "visual_intent": "Decision-routing cards and review threshold.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "stakes"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B07.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.69,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "narration_text": "A reliability diagram groups predictions into probability ranges, often called bins. For every group, compare the average predicted probability with the observed outcome frequency. The comparison asks whether a 60, 70, 80, or 90 percent group behaves like its label.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "isotype",
        "visual_intent": "Four large probability bins and comparison rule.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "bins"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B08.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.82,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "narration_text": "The diagram plots predicted probability horizontally and observed frequency vertically. A diagonal reference line represents alignment. Points below it often suggest overconfidence; points above it often suggest underconfidence. Closer alignment indicates better calibration within the evaluated data.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 21,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "drawon",
        "visual_intent": "Large reliability diagram with diagonal reference line.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "diagram"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B09.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.9,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "narration_text": "The diagonal is a reference, not a promise of perfect alignment. Finite datasets are noisy, bins may contain different numbers of examples, and small samples can fluctuate. Perfect calibration is not expected from every dataset—and would not eliminate uncertainty.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 20,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "annotate",
        "visual_intent": "Reliability diagram with finite-sample caution.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "finite"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B10.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.58,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "narration_text": "Calibration can vary across datasets, time periods, subgroups, and deployment environments. A model calibrated during evaluation may become poorly calibrated when conditions change. That is why probability quality belongs in ongoing model monitoring.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "flow",
        "visual_intent": "Environment change and monitoring cards.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "variation"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B11.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.13,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B11.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "narration_text": "Methods can recalibrate probabilities using validation data. Platt scaling, isotonic regression, and temperature scaling are examples. None is universally best; the appropriate choice depends on the model, available data, and evaluation setting.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "cards",
        "visual_intent": "Three recalibration-method cards with boundary.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "recalibration"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B12.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B12.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.87,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B12.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B13",
      "narration_text": "Calibration asks whether probabilities reflect observed frequencies. Discrimination or classification performance asks how effectively the model separates or predicts classes. A model can behave differently on these dimensions. Calibrated does not automatically mean good, and accurate does not automatically mean calibrated.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "divergence",
        "visual_intent": "Calibration versus discrimination comparison.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "dimensions"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B13.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B13.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 20.91,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B13.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ModelCalibrationExplainer",
        "at": "2026-10-16T17:42:07"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B14",
      "narration_text": "A prediction tells you what the model thinks will happen. Calibration tells you how much you should trust the probability attached to that prediction.",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "drawon",
        "visual_intent": "Final prediction-to-calibration distinction.",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ModelCalibrationExplainer",
          "props": {
            "kind": "takeaway"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B14.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-16T17:29:00"
          }
        }
      },
